#!/bin/sh
# N'applique PAS les migrations : la base est par utilisateur et n'existe pas encore ici, et
# scripts/db.ts importe server/config/env.ts, qui refuse une configuration absente. Le lanceur
# (/usr/bin/easyactions) s'en charge au premier démarrage, sous l'identité du bon utilisateur.
set -e

if [ "$1" = "configure" ]; then
  if command -v update-desktop-database >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    update-desktop-database -q /usr/share/applications 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
  if command -v gtk-update-icon-cache >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    gtk-update-icon-cache -q -f /usr/share/icons/hicolor 2>/dev/null || true
  fi

  cat <<'NEXT'

EasyActions is installed. Three steps to get going, as your normal user (not root):

  1. Start it                  easyactions
     Opens the app in its own window and creates your database on first run.

  2. Connect your GitHub App   easyactions settings:setup-code
     Prints a code valid for 30 minutes. Type it on the /setup page together
     with your GitHub App's addresses, client ID and client secret.

  3. Make yourself an admin    easyactions users:promote <your-github-login>
     Sign in through the app once first, then run this.

Other commands:
  easyactions db:status                      check the database
  easyactions settings:setup-code --reset    repoint this install at another GitHub App
  easyactions stop                           stop the background server
  easyactions help                           everything else
  systemctl --user enable --now easyactions  keep the server running across reboots

Your data lives in ~/.local/share/easyactions, your settings in ~/.config/easyactions.

NEXT
fi

exit 0
